      "abstract": "AbstractEstuaries are critical for sulfur cycling, yet the microbial basis of dimethylsulfide (DMS) production remains obscure. Here, we reconstructed 2,270 medium-to-high-quality metagenome-assembled genomes from four Chinese estuaries and identified 572 genomes encoding the H2S/methanethiol S-methylation (Mdd) pathway via MddA, MddH, and MddM enzymes. Metagenomic profiling indicated that Mdd-encoding microbes span 26 phyla, constituting an average of 15.9% and 45.5% of the reconstructed microbial communities in water and sediment, respectively. Metatranscriptomic data from global estuaries showed mddH abundance rivaled that of dimethylsulfoniopropionate (DMSP) lyase genes dddP, increased with rising salinity, and exceeded mddA in sediments. Co-occurrence networks identified Mdd-encoding microbes as one of the keystone taxa, frequently co-occurring with denitrifying microbes in sediments. Evolutionary analyses revealed purifying selection acting on mdd genes in estuaries, with higher nucleotide diversity and recombination rates observed in sediments. We also discovered MddA-like proteins in Thorarchaeia from estuarine and other diverse habitats worldwide. Collectively, our findings suggest the Mdd pathway may represent a substantial, previously underappreciated contributor to estuarine DMS production.",
      "journal": "ISME Communications",
      "doi": "10.1093/ismeco/ycag343",
