      "id": "10.1186/s42523-026-00660-8",
      "title": "Effects of dietary tannins and Asparagopsis taxiformis on lactation performance, metabolic status, rumen fermentation, and microbial ecology in early-lactation Holstein cows",
      "abstract": "Dietary feed additives that modify rumen fermentation can affect microbial ecology and cow-level performance, but responses may differ by additive type and physiological stage. This study compared dietary tannins and the red macroalga Asparagopsis taxiformis (AT) in early-lactation dairy cows. Thirty multiparous Holstein cows (48 ± 5 DIM) were assigned to a 10-d adaptation period followed by a 20-d experimental period and allocated to one of three diets: control (CON), tannin-supplemented diet (TAN; 300 g/cow per day of quebracho–chestnut tannin premix), or AT-supplemented diet (ASP; 150 g/cow per day). Tannin supplementation increased milk yield, fat-corrected milk, and energy-corrected milk, and was associated with lower serum non-esterified fatty acids (NEFA), lower β-hydroxybutyrate (BHB), and greater total antioxidant capacity. In contrast, ASP did not improve milk production and was associated with the highest NEFA concentration. Total ruminal volatile fatty acid concentration did not differ among diets, but ASP increased propionate, whereas TAN increased butyrate and n-valeric acid and reduced ruminal NH₃-N. ASP showed stronger separation from CON and TAN in bacterial and archaeal communities, lower bacterial and archaeal Shannon diversity, lower abundance of Methanosphaera spp., lower protozoal diversity, and greater dominance of Entodinium spp. In contrast, tannin supplementation produced more moderate rumen ecological changes, with bacterial and archaeal communities remaining more similar to CON. These results indicate that tannin and AT supplementation generated distinct host–rumen response profiles in early-lactation dairy cows. Tannin supplementation was associated with higher milk output and more favorable energy-metabolism and antioxidant indicators. By contrast, AT produced more pronounced microbial and predicted functional shifts, but these responses were not accompanied by improved lactational performance or metabolic status. These findings support cautious evaluation of AT use in early-lactation cows when animal-performance, metabolic, fermentation, and microbial endpoints are considered together.",
