      "id": "10.1186/s42523-026-00661-7",
      "title": "Pichia kudriavzevii supplementation alters rumen microbiome and enhances milk performance in heat-stressed dairy buffaloes",
      "abstract": "Yeast is widely used as a probiotic feed additive in ruminant nutrition. However, its effects on rumen function and milk performance in heat-stressed buffaloes remain poorly understood. To address this, we investigated the physiological and microbial responses to Pichia kudriavzevii Y8 supplementation. Twenty-seven dairy buffaloes under heat stress were randomly assigned to three groups: a control group (CON, basal diet), a low-dose supplementation group (LPK, basal diet + 2 × 1011 CFU/head/day), and a high-dose supplementation group (HPK, basal diet + 4 × 1011 CFU/head/day). Throughout the experimental period, all animals were exposed to heat stress, with the Buffalo Comfort Climatic Conditions Index consistently exceeding 34.65. The results showed that Y8 supplementation significantly lowered rectal temperature and respiratory rate, while increasing dry matter intake and the apparent digestibility of dry matter, crude protein, neutral detergent fiber, and acid detergent fiber. It simultaneously enhanced the yields of milk fat, protein, and lactose, whereas the milk yield was unchanged. Serum indicators analysis revealed elevated total protein and urea nitrogen concentrations, along with reduced triglyceride concentration in Y8 supplemented groups. Rumen fermentation parameters showed increased concentrations of acetate, propionate, and total volatile fatty acids in Y8 supplemented groups. Amplicon sequencing (16S, 18S, and ITS) revealed that Y8 altered bacterial beta-diversity and increased the relative abundance of Prevotella. Metagenomic analysis further showed that the abundance of genes involved in pyruvate metabolism (pycA, pta, and aceE), branched-chain amino acid degradation (tyrB and sad), lactate utilization (lldD), and succinate synthesis (PCCA, MCEE, and acnB) were enriched, whereas genes associated with the propionate synthesis pathway (porABD) were decreased in Y8 supplemented group. Collectively, Y8 supplementation improved milk performance in dairy buffaloes by modulating rumen microbial composition and metabolic functions, and serves as a potential candidate for dietary intervention under heat stress.",
