      "title": "Safety Evaluation and Stress Tolerance of the Evolved Probiotic Yeast Saccharomyces boulardii SBPY3_EV for Functional Food Applications",
      "abstract": "AbstractThe selection of robust probiotic strains with high gastrointestinal (GI) persistence is critical for the development of effective functional foods. Evolved yeast strain, Saccharomyces boulardii SBPY3_EV, was characterized through whole-genome sequencing (WGS) and functional phenotyping. WGS analysis confirmed high genomic integrity (99.2% BUSCO completeness) and phylogenetic identity with reference S. boulardii strains, supporting its status as a safe probiotic candidate. Safety evaluations further demonstrated non-hemolytic activity and susceptibility to clinically relevant azole antifungals. Phenotypic assays revealed that SBPY3_EV possesses superior GI fitness compared to its parental lineage and commercial standards. Evolved strain exhibited a significant 3.51-fold increase in acid tolerance (pH 3.0) and maintained robust viability in the presence of 1.5% bile salts and 9% NaCl. In vitro colonization assays using Caco-2 and HT-29 intestinal epithelial models demonstrated that SBPY3_EV achieves exceptional adhesion rates (80.4%–84.7%), significantly outperforming the reference probiotic Lacticaseibacillus rhamnosus GG. Furthermore, SBPY3_EV showed high survival (88–100%) in the gastric and during simulated small intestinal transit and exerted antagonistic activity against key foodborne pathogens, including Escherichia coli and Staphylococcus aureus. These findings provide a molecular and physiological basis for the enhanced performance of SBPY3_EV, positioning it as a highly resilient next-generation probiotic for food and nutraceutical applications.",
